.TH MODULES 10 "October 2026" "1.1" "Neverdark Manual"
.SH NAME
modules \- Install and write game modules
.SH DESCRIPTION
.PP
Neverdark is not one game. It is a boot engine plus a set of \fBmodules\fP,
one shared library each, loaded into regions. A module can add commands,
tables and event handlers that run only for the players of the region it is
loaded into, so different corners of the world can be different games.
.PP
.SS Installing a module
.PP
A module is a shared library named \fIlibnd-name\fP\fB.so\fP, installed on
the engine's library path \- the directory the engine itself lives in.
.B loadmod
loads it by bare stem, without the suffix:
.PP
.RS 4
loadmod libnd-core
.RE
.PP
A module attaches to one region, not to the whole world: you must rule the
exact region you load into (see \fBloadmod\fP(10)). The set is remembered in
the region's row and restored on every boot. \fBmodlist\fP(10) shows a
region's modules and \fBunloadmod\fP(10) takes one out; \fBgrant\fP(10) hands
a finished region to another player.
.PP
.SS Writing a module
.PP
A module is an ordinary shared object built against the engine headers. Its
contract is small:
.PP
.RS 4
.IP \(bu 2
include \fB<nd/xy.h>\fP;
.IP \(bu
export \fBXY_MODULE_API void xy_install(void)\fP for setup;
.IP \(bu
implement event hooks with \fBXY_IMPL\fP (the hook list is in \fBnd/hooks.h\fP);
.IP \(bu
export services to other modules with \fBXY_DECL\fP plus \fBXY_IMPL\fP;
.IP \(bu
write to a player with \fBnd_printf\fP, and read and write persistent tables
with \fBnd_get\fP and \fBnd_put\fP.
.RE
.PP
Dependencies are declared at install time with \fBxy_load\fP, so a module
names what it needs instead of assuming a load order. Build the result as
\fIlibnd-name\fP\fB.so\fP and place it beside the engine.
.PP
The unix subsystem, reached with \fBsh\fP(10), is the workbench: edit sources,
run the build, and reload in place.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR loadmod,unloadmod,modlist,release,grant,target,sh,begin (10)
.SH AUTHOR
quirinpa <quirinpa@tty.pt>
